=== tail
The strongest is a bound: a function $B$ with a closing rung below $B(n)$ for
every $n$ in the class. All blocks measured are consistent with
$B(n) = Cn^{1/4}$, $C$ a little above $4.6$. A bound of any shape settles the conjecture on every range it
covers, because the rungs below it are finitely many and each is decidable by the
criterion of \Cref{lem:crit}; and by \Cref{cor:squeeze} and the $k$-shift reading,
a value forcing a large rung must satisfy a growing list of independent
multiplicative conditions. That is the statement worth attacking, and it is
arithmetic about the primes of $n(n+r)/4$ rather than anything about unit
fractions.

\begin{thebibliography}{9}
\bibitem{Mordell1969} L.~J.~Mordell, \emph{Diophantine Equations}, Academic
Press, 1969, ch.~30.

\bibitem{Vaughan1970} R.~C.~Vaughan, \emph{On a problem of Erd\H{o}s, Straus and
Schinzel}, Mathematika \textbf{17} (1970), 193--198.

\bibitem{ElsholtzTao} C.~Elsholtz and T.~Tao, \emph{Counting the number of
solutions to the Erd\H{o}s--Straus equation on unit fractions}, J.\ Aust.\ Math.\
Soc.\ \textbf{94} (2013), 50--105.

\bibitem{Schinzel} A.~Schinzel, \emph{Sur quelques propri\'et\'es des nombres
$3/n$ et $4/n$, o\`u $n$ est un nombre impair}, Mathesis \textbf{65} (1956),
219--222.

\bibitem{Guy} R.~K.~Guy, \emph{Unsolved Problems in Number Theory}, 3rd ed.,
Springer, 2004, problem D11.
\end{thebibliography}

